            <ArticleTitle>Controlled-release morphine tablets. A double-blind trial in dental surgery patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1259-64</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a randomized double-blind comparison of controlled-release morphine tablets (MST-1; 2 x 10 mg) and oral morphine sulphate in solution (20 mg) in 28 patients (20 females) who had undergone removal of impacted lower third molars or a dental clearance under general anaesthetic. The response in both groups was very poor: eight of 15 patients in MST-1 group and six of 13 patients in the standard group required &quot;rescue&quot; analgesics and were withdrawn from the study within the first 2h. No threshold plasma concentration of morphine corresponding to a particular analgesic effect was apparent. MST-1 produced significantly greater plasma concentrations at 8h compared with the standard preparation. Controlled-release morphine, or any oral formulation of morphine, may not be suitable for the treatment of acute pain after operation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
